The '''Turung language''' is an endangered [[Sino-Tibetan languages|Sino-Tibetan language]], closely related to [[Singpho dialect|Singpho]],<ref>{{Cite book |last=Morey |first=Stephen |chapter=The Tai Languages of Assam |title=The Tai-Kadai languages |editor1=Anthony V. N. Diller |editor2=Jerold A. Edmondson |editor3=Yongxian Luo |series=Routledge language family series |publisher=Routledge |date=2008 |pages=207-253 [211] |quote=Turung today is a Tibeto-Burman language of the Jinghpaw sub-family, and is called Turung by its speakers.}}</ref> spoken in seven villages in central [[Assam]]. Many [[Turung people]] now speak [[Assamese language|Assamese]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Morey |first=Stephen |chapter=The Tai Languages of Assam |title=The Tai-Kadai languages |editor1=Anthony V. N. Diller |editor2=Jerold A. Edmondson |editor3=Yongxian Luo |series=Routledge language family series |publisher=Routledge |date=2008 |pages=207-253 [211]}}</ref><ref name="Morey 2006">{{Cite web |title=Singpho Language of North East India (including Turung) {{!}} Endangered Languages Archive |url=https://www.elararchive.org/dk0006/ |access-date=2022-12-20 |website=www.elararchive.org}}</ref>

The total population of the ethnic group is over 30,000, and they primarily live in the [[Jorhat]], [[Golaghat]] and [[Karbi Anglong]] districts of [[Assam]].

==Possible Tai language existence==
The ancestors of the modern Turung people possibly spoke a [[Tai languages|Tai language]] that was called Turung or Tairong and is now extinct.<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |title=The Tai Languages of Assam |encyclopedia=The Tai-Kadai Languages |last=Morey |first=Stephen |year=2004 |doi=10.4324/9780203641873}}</ref> The modern Turung language is influenced by Tai languages.

